# eForte Solutions - Project Rules & Best Practices

## Technology Stack
- **Framework**: Next.js 16 (App Router)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4
- **Language**: TypeScript
- **React Version**: React 19

## Code Organization & Architecture

### Component Structure
- **Component-based architecture**: Break down UI into reusable, well-organized components
- **Atomic Design Pattern**: Use a layered component architecture
- **File organization**: 
  - Create components in a `components/` directory
  - Group related components in subdirectories (e.g., `components/ui/`, `components/atoms/`, `components/sections/`)
  - Use PascalCase for component file names (e.g., `Button.tsx`, `Header.tsx`)

### Component Architecture Layers

#### `components/ui/` - Shadcn Base Components
- **Purpose**: Store base shadcn/ui components (third-party library components)
- **Content**: Raw shadcn components installed via `npx shadcn@latest add [component]`
- **Examples**: `button.tsx`, `input.tsx`, `card.tsx`, `dialog.tsx`
- **Rules**: 
  - Do NOT modify these components directly
  - Keep them as-is from shadcn/ui
  - These are the foundation/base components

#### `components/atoms/` - Customized Components
- **Purpose**: Store customized versions of shadcn components
- **Content**: Custom components built on top of shadcn components from `ui/`
- **Examples**: `Button1.tsx`, `Button2.tsx`, `Input1.tsx`, `Card1.tsx`
- **Rules**:
  - Import and extend shadcn components from `components/ui/`
  - Apply custom styling, props, or functionality
  - Each variant gets its own file (e.g., `Button1.tsx`, `Button2.tsx`)
  - Use descriptive names that indicate the variant or use case

### Component Best Practices
- **Single Responsibility**: Each component should have a single, well-defined purpose
- **Reusability**: Design components to be reusable across the application
- **Props Interface**: Always define TypeScript interfaces for component props
- **Default Exports**: Use default exports for components
- **Component Naming**: Use descriptive, semantic names that reflect the component's purpose
- **Page Sections**: ALL page sections must be created as separate components in `components/sections/` and imported into page files
  - Do NOT write section code directly in page files
  - Each major section (Banner, Hero, Features, etc.) should be its own component
  - Page files should only import and compose section components
  - Example: `<Banner />`, `<TrustedBySection />`, `<LegacyToIntelligenceSection />`

### Code Quality Standards
- **Refactoring**: Keep code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) - extract common logic into utilities or hooks
- **Readability**: Write code that is self-documenting with clear variable and function names
- **Type Safety**: Use TypeScript strictly - avoid `any` types, define proper interfaces
- **Comments**: Add comments for complex logic, but prefer self-documenting code

### Styling Guidelines
- **Tailwind CSS**: Use Tailwind utility classes for styling
- **Responsive Design**: Use Tailwind's responsive breakpoints (sm, md, lg, xl, 2xl)
- **Consistency**: Maintain consistent spacing, colors, and typography using Tailwind's design tokens
- **Custom Styles**: When needed, extend Tailwind in `globals.css` using CSS variables

### Custom Color Tokens
- **`text-desc`**: `#A0A0C0` - Use for descriptive/secondary text
- **`text-white`**: `#ffffff` - White text color
- **`text-primary-pink`**: `#D3287A` - Primary pink color for text
- **`bg-default`**: `#0A0A1A` - Default background color
- **Usage**: These colors are defined in `globals.css` and can be used as Tailwind utility classes (e.g., `text-desc`, `bg-default`)

### Container Component
- **Standard Container**: Use `Container` component from `@/components/atoms/Container` for all content sections
- **Max Width**: Container has a max-width of 1400px and is centered with responsive padding
- **Exceptions**: Do NOT use Container for:
  - Carousel components (should be full-width)
  - Banner/Hero images (should be full-width)
  - Any element that needs to break out of the container width
- **Usage**: Wrap content sections with `<Container>` to maintain consistent width across the site

### File Structure Example
```
app/
  layout.tsx
  page.tsx
  globals.css
components/
  ui/          # Shadcn base components (button.tsx, input.tsx, etc.)
  atoms/       # Customized components (Button1.tsx, Button2.tsx, Input1.tsx, etc.)
  sections/    # Page sections (header, footer, hero, etc.)
  features/    # Feature-specific components
lib/          # Utility functions and helpers
types/        # TypeScript type definitions
```

### Component Usage Pattern
```typescript
// components/ui/button.tsx (shadcn component - do not modify)
// This is installed via: npx shadcn@latest add button

// components/atoms/Button1.tsx (customized version)
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

export interface Button1Props extends React.ComponentProps<typeof Button> {
  // Add custom props if needed
}

export default function Button1({ className, ...props }: Button1Props) {
  return (
    <Button
      className={cn("custom-styles-here", className)}
      {...props}
    />
  );
}
```

### Design Implementation
- **Figma to Code**: When implementing Figma designs:
  - Match spacing, colors, and typography exactly
  - Ensure responsive behavior matches design specifications
  - Pay attention to hover states, transitions, and animations
  - Maintain pixel-perfect accuracy where possible

### Performance
- **Image Optimization**: Use Next.js Image component for all images
- **Code Splitting**: Leverage Next.js automatic code splitting
- **Lazy Loading**: Implement lazy loading for below-the-fold content when appropriate

### Accessibility
- **Semantic HTML**: Use semantic HTML elements
- **ARIA Labels**: Add ARIA labels where necessary for screen readers
- **Keyboard Navigation**: Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible

## Workflow
- Always follow these rules when working on this project
- Maintain consistency with existing code patterns
- Refactor when code duplication is detected
- Test components in isolation before integration
